    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vqwnams",
      "content": "Evolution is the gradual change in heritable characteristics of populations over successive generations. It explains the origin of diversity of life on Earth. The term evolution means 'descent with modification'."
    },
    {
      "id": "xbb4zml",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "sih6069",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_sih6069.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "id": "m5q3lw6",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "lmyykc7",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_lmyykc7.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "id": "pwtnzeo",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Origin of Life – Basic Concepts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g3a7sfo",
      "content": "The origin of life is explained by the chemical evolution theory proposed by Oparin and Haldane. According to this theory, life originated from non-living organic molecules in primitive Earth conditions."
    },
    {
      "id": "ms533sc",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Theories Before Darwin"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g9d263i",
      "content": "Before Darwin, Lamarck proposed the theory of inheritance of acquired characters. He suggested that traits acquired during lifetime are passed to offspring."
    },
    {
      "id": "3wf57qe",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Scientist</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Main Idea</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Lamarck</p>",
            "<p>Inheritance of acquired characters</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Darwin</p>",
            "<p>Natural selection</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "lxn010h",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Darwin’s Theory of Evolution"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ksbluxu",
      "content": "Charles Darwin proposed the theory of Natural Selection in his book 'On the Origin of Species' (1859). He observed variation among organisms during his voyage on HMS Beagle."
    },
    {
      "id": "xxrx3k6",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Main Postulates of Darwinism"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "o10ye1x",
      "content": "• Overproduction – Organisms produce more offspring than can survive."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nc5k2qx",
      "content": "• Struggle for existence – Competition for limited resources."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8xg5pif",
      "content": "• Variation – Individuals differ from each other."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4iokqru",
      "content": "• Survival of the fittest – Only best adapted survive."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dnbk9ys",
      "content": "• Natural selection – Nature selects advantageous traits."
    },
    {
      "id": "po4otjw",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Natural Selection"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fm6mwkv",
      "content": "Natural selection is the process by which individuals with favorable variations survive and reproduce. Over time, beneficial traits become more common in the population."
    },
    {
      "id": "7z4y3cb",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Example</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Directional Selection</p>",
            "<p>Peppered moth</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Stabilizing Selection</p>",
            "<p>Human birth weight</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Disruptive Selection</p>",
            "<p>Different beak sizes</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "24mrvvc",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Variation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ghcx6jf",
      "content": "Variations are differences among individuals of a species. They arise due to mutation, recombination and environmental factors."
    },
    {
      "id": "xaizn0m",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "8. Mutation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nb7qy9m",
      "content": "Mutation is a sudden heritable change in DNA sequence. Mutations are the raw material for evolution."
    },
    {
      "id": "ol3srja",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "9. Speciation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "s6qjtmz",
      "content": "Speciation is the formation of new species from existing ones. It occurs due to accumulation of variations and reproductive isolation."
    },
    {
      "id": "cvmpclh",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Type of Speciation</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Description</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Allopatric</p>",
            "<p>Due to geographical isolation</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Sympatric</p>",
            "<p>Without geographical isolation</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Artificial</p>",
            "<p>Human-controlled breeding</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "o6qq168",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "10. Evidence of Evolution"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0utwux8",
      "content": "Evolution is supported by fossil records, homologous organs, analogous organs and vestigial organs."
    },
    {
      "id": "5y1uzou",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "11. Fossil Evidence"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0y37v2e",
      "content": "Fossils are preserved remains of ancient organisms. They show gradual changes over geological time."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Lamarckism",
        "Darwinism"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Primary Premise",
          "Use and disuse of organs causes structural changes",
          "Natural selection acts on useful variations"
        ],
        [
          "Inheritance",
          "Acquired characters are passed to offspring",
          "Only pre-existing genetic variations are inherited"
        ],
        [
          "Struggle for Existence",
          "Does not consider struggle for existence",
          "Struggle for existence is vital to survival of the fittest"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "4ra7875"
    },
    {
